  input-precision-validation: LANDED 2026-07-10 with
    missing-incorporate-matrix-interface (de67cbb). dbartsData warns
    when range(y)/max(abs(y)) < 1e-10 (0/0 guarded); deliberately NO
    cardinality check backs it up - a genuine double-precision
    collapse always trips the ratio, while anything cardinality-only
    is well-separated legitimate discrete data (subsumption argument
    in small-validation-fixes.md). GP predict-vs-recorded-fit note
    landed in man/dbartsPriors.Rd and gp-leaves.md.
  multi-forest-models: multinomial, heteroscedastic, hurdle - the BCF
    two-forest machinery (forest-split-bcf, complete at c9fd2fe) is
    the template; one design note each when picked. Review-6
    prerequisites: forest-combiner FIRST (all three bend on it);
    heteroscedastic additionally needs a fourth leaf kind
    (multiplicative-positive, non-integrable) plus the non-conjugate
    MoveStrategy the design anticipated but never built, and a
    decision on per-observation residual variance (fold into the
    workingWeights channel vs a sigma vector).
  chi-default-research: staged by VD 2026-07-08 alongside the
    chi-hyperprior-df fix. The chi(1.25, Inf) binary default came
    from a simulation showing good binary-outcome performance; with
    the label now honest (chi(1.5)), re-derive the default from
    evidence. PART 1 DONE (roadmap-survey rider, 2026-07-10,
    detailed table in roadmap-survey.md): dbarts is the ONLY
    package with a hyperprior-on-k binary default; the field is
    fixed k=2 (BART, bartMachine, bartz), SoftBart alone at fixed
    k=1 (unexplained), stochtree samples leaf VARIANCE via IG (not
    k), pymc-bart exposes no knob; NO published rationale anywhere
    for a non-2 default. PART 2 remains: a broad simulation over
    binary scenarios (n, p, prevalence, signal strength/sparsity,
    friedman + linear + interaction DGPs) comparing k defaults and
    chi hyperprior settings on held-out log-loss, AUC, calibration,
    and interval coverage - now also deciding the chi-k-runaway
    remedy's default-scale half and rbart_vi's k default (interface
    taste call T5). Output: a design note + recommendation; any
    default change is its own posterior-changing item with VD's
    sign-off.
  bcf-glue-sigma-calibration: sbc-calibration (review 4) finding,
    tier A. Standard BCF (n=200) with the glue UPDATING shows a
    systematic sigma miscalibration under SBC - posterior sigma too
    large (rank mean 63.6 vs 75, ecdf 0.134 vs band 0.092, chisqP
    0.000 at thin=120/burn=18000, chosen from measured |a| ACF).
    LOCALIZED: the fixed-glue control (updateA=updateB=FALSE, same
    everything) passes ALL functionals, so the two-forest backfit is
    exact and the defect is in the glue-on path only. The glue full
    conditionals verified algebraically exact (consistent with audit
    block 6). Prior-dominant n=40 config PASSES sigma dead-centre -
    an algebra error would show MORE when the prior dominates, so
    the n-pattern favors SLOW MIXING along the (a, mu-amplitude)
    scale ridge (a*mu invariant under (a/c, c*mu); its relaxation
    time can far exceed the |a| marginal ACF) over an exactness gap.
    Next step: a targeted experiment - (1) rerun the n=200 config at
    several multiples of chain length to see whether the sigma rank
    bias shrinks toward zero (mixing) or plateaus (defect); (2) if
    mixing confirmed, the standard remedy is an ancillarity-
    sufficiency interweaving (ASIS) or joint rescaling move on the
    (a, mu) ridge - posterior-preserving, its own gated item; (3) if
    plateau, deep-dive the glue-scaled forest update path. Repro:
    runSbcBCF in benchmarks/R/sbc.R (rides the review-4 landing).
